[
  {
    "content": "# Demo - Aider Conventions

> Load these conventions in Aider with \`aider --read CONVENTIONS.md\`, or add \`read: CONVENTIONS.md\` to \`.aider.conf.yml\`.

## Project Overview

**Description**: A demo project

**Visibility**: Public repository

## Technology Stack

### Languages
- typescript
- python

### Frameworks & Libraries
- nextjs

### Database
- postgresql

## Development Guidelines

### Workflow Rules
- Always run and test locally after making changes

### Commands
- Build: \`npm run build\`
- Test: \`npm test\`

### CI/CD & Infrastructure
- **CI/CD Platform**: GitHub Actions

### Git Workflow
- Never commit directly to \`main\`; work on a branch and open a pull request

## Best Practices

- **Write clean code**: Prioritize readability and maintainability
- **Handle errors properly**: Don't ignore errors, handle them appropriately
- **Consider security**: Review code for potential security vulnerabilities

## Custom Instructions

The project owner has provided these additional instructions:

Prefer small pull requests.


## Repository Files to Generate

When requested or when missing, create these files following these guidelines:

- This repository uses the **MIT** license. If the \`LICENSE\` file doesn't exist yet, please create it with copyright holder: Tester.

## ⚠️ Security Notice

> **Do not commit secrets to the repository or to the live app.**
> Always use secure standards to transmit sensitive information.
> Use environment variables, secret managers, or secure vaults for credentials.

**🔍 Security Audit Recommendation:** When making changes that involve authentication, data handling, API endpoints, or dependencies, proactively offer to perform a security review of the affected code.",
    "fileName": "CONVENTIONS.md",
  },
]
`;

exports[`web generator golden output > aider with every answer 1`] = `
[
  {
    "content": "# Demo - Aider Conventions

> Load these conventions in Aider with \`aider --read CONVENTIONS.md\`, or add \`read: CONVENTIONS.md\` to \`.aider.conf.yml\`.

## Before Starting Any Coding Task

1. Check existing worktrees with \`git worktree list\` and create a new one for this task if needed
2. Use the naming convention: \`git worktree add -b ai/[task-description] ../[repo-name]-ai-[task-description]\`
3. Navigate to the worktree directory before making any changes
4. Commit changes when the task is finished. Merge to main, and clean the worktree.

## Project Overview

**Description**: A demo project

**Architecture Pattern**: Hexagonal / Ports & Adapters - domain at center, adapters at edges
**Visibility**: Public repository
**Development OS**: macOS, Windows

### Repository
- **URL**: https://github.com/acme/demo
- **Platform**: GitHub

### Reference Materials
- **Example Repository**: https://github.com/acme/example
- **External Documentation**: https://docs.acme.test

## Technology Stack

### Languages
- typescript
- python

### Frameworks & Libraries
- nextjs

### Database
- postgresql

### Tooling
- **Package manager**: pnpm. Use it for every install and script.
- **Runtime**: Node.js
- **Monorepo tool**: Turborepo
- **ORM / database toolkit**: prisma

### AI Technology Selection
For technologies beyond those listed, analyze the codebase and suggest appropriate solutions.

## Development Guidelines

### Communication Style
- Be concise and direct
- Developer background: backend developer — adapt responses to this domain expertise
- Skill level: Expert

### Workflow Rules
- Always run and test locally after making changes
- Always verify your work before returning: run tests, check builds, confirm changes work as expected
- When you learn new project patterns or conventions, suggest updates to this configuration file
- **Plan first** for complex, multi-step or risky changes
- **Explanations**: concise, to the point
- **Accessibility**: follow WCAG; use semantic markup, labels, keyboard access and enough contrast
- **Performance**: keep an eye on load time, memory and needless work; measure before optimising
- When stuck, **stop and ask** rather than attempting workarounds

### MCP Servers

The developer has these MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers available. Use them when relevant:

- github
- postgres

### Infrastructure

- **Server access**: via SSH. SSH key: \`~/.ssh/deploy\`
- **Deployment**: Docker Compose (manual)

### Important Files to Read First
Before making changes, read these files to understand the project:
- README.md
- ARCHITECTURE.md
- docs/adr

### Project Context
- **STRICT MODE**: This is a work project. Follow procedures exactly as documented.
- Do not deviate from established patterns or make assumptions.
- Always ask for clarification rather than guessing.
- Document all decisions and changes thoroughly.

### Commands
- Build: \`npm run build\`
- Test: \`npm test\`
- Lint: \`npm run lint\`
- Dev: \`npm run dev\`
- **Type check**: \`npm run typecheck\`
- **Format**: \`npm run format\`
- **Pre-commit hooks**: \`npx lint-staged\`
- **Clean**: \`rm -rf dist\`
- \`npm run db:migrate\`

### Code Style & Conventions
- **Naming**: PascalCase for classes and types (e.g., MyClass, UserProfile)
- **Error Handling**: Result / Either types (no exceptions for control flow)
- **Logging**: structured
- **File length**: keep files under 400 lines; split larger ones
- **Imports**: group imports: external packages, then internal modules, then relative paths
- **Comment language**: write code comments in english
- **Documentation comments**: TSDoc
- **Additional Notes**: Prefer pure functions.

### Boundaries & Permissions

**✅ ALWAYS do (no need to ask)**:
- Run tests

**❓ ASK first before doing**:
- Add dependencies

**🚫 NEVER do (strictly prohibited)**:
- Push to main

### Testing Requirements
**Testing Levels**:
- Unit tests (individual functions/methods)
- End-to-end tests (complete user flows)
- smoke
**Frameworks**: vitest, playwright
**Coverage Target**: 90% minimum
**Notes**: Mock the network.
- **Test-driven development**: write a failing test first, then the code that makes it pass
- **Snapshot tests**: use snapshot tests for stable output such as rendered components
- **Mocking**: mock collaborators so each unit is tested in isolation

### CI/CD & Infrastructure
- **CI/CD Platform**: GitHub Actions
- **Deployment Targets**: Vercel, Kubernetes
- **Container Builds**: Project builds Docker/OCI container images
- **Container Registry**: GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io)
- **Published images**:
  - \`acme/demo\`
  - \`acme/demo-worker\`

### Git Workflow
- **Branching**: GitHub Flow: short-lived feature branches merged into the default branch through pull requests
- **Default branch**: \`develop\`
- Never commit directly to \`develop\`; work on a branch and open a pull request
- Sign every commit (GPG or SSH)
- **Version tags**: \`v1.2.3\`
- **Changelog**: managed with Changesets

## Best Practices

- **Write clean code**: Prioritize readability and maintainability
- **Handle errors properly**: Don't ignore errors, handle them appropriately
- **Consider security**: Review code for potential security vulnerabilities
- **Conventional commits**: Use conventional commit messages (feat:, fix:, docs:, chore:, refactor:, test:, style:)
- **Semantic versioning**: Follow semver (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) for version numbers
- **Dependency updates**: Keep dependencies updated (Dependabot) configured)

## Custom Instructions

The project owner has provided these additional instructions:

Prefer small pull requests.

## Self-Improving Configuration

This file should evolve as we work together:
1. Track coding patterns and preferences
2. Note corrections made to suggestions
3. Update periodically with learned preferences


## Repository Files to Generate

When requested or when missing, create these files following these guidelines:

- **\`.editorconfig\`**: Generate with standard settings for consistent code formatting across editors (indent style, line endings, trim trailing whitespace).
- **\`CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\`**: Generate based on the Contributor Covenant or similar community standards document.
- **\`SECURITY.md\`**: Generate with security policy, supported versions, and instructions for reporting vulnerabilities privately.
- **\`.gitignore\`**: Generate for typescript, python, nextjs. Include IDE files, build artifacts, dependencies, and environment files.
- **\`.dockerignore\`**: Generate for typescript, python, nextjs. Exclude .git, dependencies, .env files, build artifacts, and documentation to keep images small.
- **\`ROADMAP.md\`**: Create and maintain with planned features, milestones, and future ideas. Organize by timeline (Q1, Q2, etc.) or priority. Keep updated as the project evolves.
- This repository uses the **MIT** license. If the \`LICENSE\` file doesn't exist yet, please create it with copyright holder: Test Author. Additional licensing notes: Docs are CC-BY.

